# Copy to .env and fill in your system. Never commit .env.
#
# Four ways to log on, three of them without a password:
#   kerberos    (default) SPNEGO SSO with the logged-on Windows user's ticket
#   certificate an X.509 client certificate, for service and technical users
#               that have no Kerberos identity
#   oauth       an OAuth 2.0 bearer token, for an SAP BTP ABAP environment or a
#               system that publishes ADT behind SOAUTH2
#   password    the last resort, and the only one that can lock a SAP user
# See docs/Authentication.md.

# Required
SAP_URL=https://your-sap-server.example.com:44301
SAP_USER=YOUR_SAP_USER

# Optional, but recommended
SAP_CLIENT=100
SAP_LANGUAGE=EN

# The system id, as in sy-sysid. Optional, and worth setting whenever more than
# one of these servers is registered: it is announced at connect time, so a client
# asked to "check something in DEV client 200" can pick the right server without
# calling anything. A server cannot switch system or client at runtime.
# It is also checked against the system actually logged on to - SAP names it in
# the session cookie - and a disagreement is reported by healthcheck.
SAP_SYSTEM_ID=DEV

# Optional: accept a certificate issued by an internal/unknown CA. Development only.
NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0

# --- Tool profile -----------------------------------------------------------
# Which tools this server lists, and therefore which it will answer. Unset means
# `all`, so an existing setup is unchanged.
#
#   core       9 tools, ~2,737 tokens   read a system and complete one edit
#   analyst   18 tools, ~3,982 tokens   read-only: dictionary, data, RFC calls
#   dev       49 tools, ~8,034 tokens   the edit cycle plus tests, ATC, transports
#   all      129 tools, ~17,759 tokens  everything (default; 116 on DEV after gating)
#
# Counts are what tools/list returns, healthcheck included — it sits outside every
# profile, because it is the tool that answers "which profile am I running?".
#
# The number that matters is the second one: a client that cannot fetch tool
# schemas on demand pays it on every turn. Claude Code defers them and should use
# `all`; a small local model with a 128k window should not.
#
# A tool outside the profile is not listed *and* not callable — which is what
# makes `analyst` a guarantee that nothing edits source, not just a smaller menu.
# An unknown name here stops the server at startup rather than falling back.
#
# `core` is small because one tool, editAbapSource, is the whole write cycle:
# lock, write, activate, unlock, releasing the lock even when a step fails. The
# four separate steps stay in `dev` and `all` for edits that need them apart.
# ABAP_MCP_PROFILE=all

# Ceiling on a single answer, in bytes. Unset follows the profile — core 24000,
# analyst 32000, dev 48000, all unlimited. 0 removes it.
#
# The tool list is a fixed cost a profile can shrink; an answer is unbounded, and
# on `core` the whole tool list is ~9KB while one adtDiscovery is ~42KB. An answer
# over the ceiling is withheld and replaced by valid JSON saying what was dropped
# and what to do instead — never by a cut-off fragment, which would not parse.
# ABAP_MCP_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES=0

# Start even when SAP refuses this user the ADT node.
#
# For a technical user that has RFC authorisations but no S_DEVELOP: SAP serves
# it /sap/gw/jsonrpc perfectly and refuses /sap/bc/adt/* outright, so the logon
# fails and the server dies at startup — taking the RFC tools with it, which
# would have worked. With this on, the session is taken from an ICF node the
# user does reach, and the RFC tools work while every ADT tool fails at call
# time. healthcheck reports `adtAvailable: false` and status `degraded`.
#
# Off by default: a logon failure is a diagnosis, and turning it into "some
# tools error later" hides it. Pair with ABAP_MCP_PROFILE=rfc, which lists only
# the tools that can still answer.
#
# The proper fix is S_DEVELOP — see docs/Authentication.md.
# ABAP_MCP_RFC_FALLBACK=0

# Which node that fallback logs on to. It only has to be one this user reaches
# that issues a session cookie and a CSRF token; the OData catalog service is
# the one such a user reliably has.
# SAP_FALLBACK_BOOTSTRAP_PATH=/sap/opu/odata/IWFND/CATALOGSERVICE;v=2/

# Ask the system what it supports before listing tools, and withhold those its
# ADT release does not expose. On DEV that is the 10 abapGit tools and the 3
# service-binding tools, which would otherwise answer 400. Costs one discovery
# call on first use; any failure leaves every tool listed. Set to off to skip it.
# ABAP_MCP_GATE=on

# --- Certificate mode -------------------------------------------------------
# Setting SAP_CERT_FILE is enough to select it; SAP_AUTH_MODE only has to be set
# to force kerberos while a certificate is configured.
# SAP_USER must then be the user CERTRULE maps the certificate to — it is not
# transmitted, but it is what the server reports as the current user.
#
# SAP_AUTH_MODE=certificate
#
# PKCS#12 (.p12/.pfx) or PEM holding the client certificate. The extension
# decides how it is opened. Note sapgenpse's export_p12 writes a legacy
# PKCS#12 that OpenSSL 3 refuses — see docs/Authentication.md §3.
# SAP_CERT_FILE=C:\Users\svc_agent\SNC\sec\claudeagent.p12
#
# PKCS#12 password / PEM key passphrase. For a PSE export this is the PSE PIN.
# SAP_CERT_PASSPHRASE=
#
# Only when the private key is in a separate file from the certificate.
# SAP_CERT_KEY_FILE=
#
# CA bundle for verifying SAP's own certificate, instead of switching TLS
# verification off.
# SAP_CA_FILE=

# --- OAuth 2.0 mode ---------------------------------------------------------
# For a system that issues bearer tokens: an SAP BTP ABAP environment, where
# there is no Kerberos realm and no ICM to configure for client certificates, or
# an on-premise system whose Basis team published ADT through SOAUTH2 so that no
# service password has to be handed out.
#
# Setting SAP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID (or SAP_OAUTH_TOKEN, or SAP_OAUTH_TOKEN_URL)
# selects the mode. A configured certificate still wins over it.
#
# The token is used once, to log on: after that the SAP session cookie carries
# every request, exactly as in the other modes. So a token that expires in five
# minutes is not a problem, and the token is minted again only when the session
# itself has to be re-established.
#
# The token endpoint. On AS ABAP it is /sap/bc/sec/oauth2/token; on SAP BTP it is
# the 'url' from the service key with /oauth/token appended. https, always.
# SAP_OAUTH_TOKEN_URL=https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
#
# The client registered in SOAUTH2, or 'clientid' from a BTP service key.
# SAP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=sb-abap-agent!t1234
#
# Its secret. Note this is a credential with the same hazard as a password: an
# OAuth 2.0 client on AS ABAP is a user in SU01, so a wrong secret counts against
# login/fails_to_user_lock. A refusal here is never retried, for that reason.
# SAP_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# How the token is obtained. Unset means client_credentials, which is the one
# that runs unattended. SAP_OAUTH_TOKEN selects 'static' and
# SAP_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN selects 'refresh_token' by themselves.
#
# The authorization_code flow is deliberately absent: it needs a browser and a
# redirect URI, and a server started by an MCP client over stdio has neither.
# Complete it once by hand and pass the refresh token in.
# SAP_OAUTH_GRANT=client_credentials
#
# Optional. Unset asks for the client's default scopes, which is usually right.
# In SOAUTH2 a scope is bound to an ICF service, so a token minted for another
# service is refused by ADT with a 401 that looks like a mapping problem.
# SAP_OAUTH_SCOPE=
#
# The long-lived half of an authorization_code flow someone completed in a
# browser. A server that rotates them - BTP does - invalidates this one on first
# use; the replacement is held in memory only, so after a restart that lapsed it
# has to be renewed by hand.
# SAP_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=
#
# An access token minted somewhere else (cf oauth-token, a proxy, a test), used
# as-is. Nothing here can renew it, so a rejection is final until the server is
# restarted with a new one.
# SAP_OAUTH_TOKEN=
#
# How the client identifies itself at the token endpoint: 'basic' (the default,
# and what SAP and UAA expect) or 'post', which puts the id and secret in the
# form body. Only worth changing when a server rejects a secret that is right.
# SAP_OAUTH_CLIENT_AUTH=basic
#
# SAP_CA_FILE applies here too, and in this mode it is *added* to the public
# roots rather than replacing them - the token endpoint is frequently a different
# host from SAP_URL, with a certificate from a public CA.

# --- Password mode ----------------------------------------------------------
# The last resort, for a system that offers neither Kerberos nor certificates.
#
# It is not interchangeable with the other two. A missing ticket or an unmapped
# certificate is refused and nothing else happens; a *wrong* password counts
# against login/fails_to_user_lock, and the account it locks is shared with
# everything else that uses that user. A typo here is an outage for other
# people, which is not true of any other setting in this file.
#
# So: prefer SAP_CERT_FILE for anything unattended. The server refuses to retry
# a rejected password — one failed logon, latched, however many tools are
# called — but it cannot undo the first one.
#
# Setting SAP_PASSWORD selects the mode. A configured certificate or OAuth client
# wins over it - the order runs from the credential that cannot lock a user to
# the one that can. https is required: Basic is base64, which is encoding, not
# encryption.
#
# The OAuth password grant reads this variable too, and carries the same hazard.
# SAP_PASSWORD=

# --- Kerberos mode ----------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: curl binary with SPNEGO support, if not the Windows system one
# (default: C:\Windows\System32\curl.exe).
# SSO_CURL_PATH=C:\Windows\System32\curl.exe

# --- Kerberos in a container -------------------------------------------------
# Only when running the Docker image in kerberos mode. A container has no host
# session to borrow a ticket from, so the realm and the credential are handed in.
# Ignored everywhere else. See docs/Authentication.md §7.
#
# The realm, and the KDC when it has no DNS SRV record. For Active Directory the
# realm is the domain name in upper case. Not needed if the host's /etc/krb5.conf
# is mounted into the container instead.
# SAP_KRB_REALM=CORP.EXAMPLE.COM
# SAP_KRB_KDC=dc01.corp.example.com
#
# The keytab, mounted read-only, and which principal in it to use. This is the
# only credential that works unattended, and the only one that outlives the
# ticket lifetime — GSS re-acquires from it when the cached ticket expires.
# A keytab is a password in another form: treat it like the .p12.
# SAP_KRB_KEYTAB=/krb5/agent.keytab
# SAP_KRB_PRINCIPAL=SVC_AGENT@CORP.EXAMPLE.COM
#
# Instead of a keytab: a ticket cache the host already holds, which expires with
# it. Linux hosts only — Windows keeps its TGT in the LSA cache, which cannot be
# written to a file. From Windows, use certificate mode.
# KRB5CCNAME=FILE:/krb5/ccache

# Optional: ICF path of the SAP Gateway JSON-RPC service, if published under an alias
# (default: /sap/gw/jsonrpc).
# SAP_JSONRPC_PATH=/sap/gw/jsonrpc
